1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,691
Why does your cat knock things off the table?

2
00:00:02,691 --> 00:00:05,364
You watch it happen and it almost looks planned.

3
00:00:05,364 --> 00:00:08,587
One paw reaches out and the cup creeps toward the edge.

4
00:00:08,587 --> 00:00:11,759
So what on earth is going on inside that little head?

5
00:00:12,359 --> 00:00:14,556
We'll talk more about that in this video.

6
00:00:15,759 --> 00:00:17,013
It's late in the evening

7
00:00:17,013 --> 00:00:19,366
and you've just made yourself a cup of tea.

8
00:00:19,366 --> 00:00:21,180
You set it down on the coffee table

9
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:23,358
and turn away for maybe one second.

10
00:00:23,358 --> 00:00:26,425
When you look back the cat is sitting right beside the mug.

11
00:00:26,425 --> 00:00:29,506
It isn't drinking the tea and it isn't even sniffing it.

12
00:00:29,506 --> 00:00:32,226
It's just looking at you. Then a single paw lifts up

13
00:00:32,226 --> 00:00:34,252
and rests against the side of the mug.

14
00:00:34,252 --> 00:00:37,357
Slowly the mug begins to slide toward the edge.

15
00:00:37,357 --> 00:00:40,806
The cat keeps its eyes locked on yours the entire time.

16
00:00:40,806 --> 00:00:43,405
And then over the side it goes. If you share a home

17
00:00:43,405 --> 00:00:46,576
with a cat you've almost certainly seen some version of this.

18
00:00:46,576 --> 00:00:49,880
Pens roll off desks and keys vanish under the sofa.

19
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:52,688
Earrings go missing from the bedside table.

20
00:00:52,688 --> 00:00:56,170
Houseplants end up on the floor with soil sprayed everywhere.

21
00:00:56,170 --> 00:00:59,920
The internet is stuffed with videos of cats doing exactly this.

22
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:01,446
And in nearly every one of them

23
00:01:01,446 --> 00:01:04,302
the cat looks completely calm about the whole thing.

24
00:01:04,302 --> 00:01:07,682
So is your cat a tiny furry villain doing it on purpose?

25
00:01:07,682 --> 00:01:10,113
Or is something else going on here?

26
00:01:10,113 --> 00:01:13,435
The answer turns out to be a few things tangled together.

27
00:01:13,435 --> 00:01:14,803
Some of it is very old

28
00:01:14,803 --> 00:01:16,849
and some of it is really about you.

29
00:01:16,849 --> 00:01:19,132
Let's start with the oldest part.

30
00:01:19,132 --> 00:01:22,091
Your house cat comes from a small wild cat that lived

31
00:01:22,091 --> 00:01:25,337
in the deserts and grasslands of Africa and the Middle East.

32
00:01:25,337 --> 00:01:29,079
That wild cat lived on mice and birds and lizards and bugs.

33
00:01:29,079 --> 00:01:31,996
To stay alive it had to hunt an awful lot.

34
00:01:31,996 --> 00:01:34,103
A small cat can't catch one big meal

35
00:01:34,103 --> 00:01:35,660
and then rest for a week.

36
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:38,648
It needs lots of little meals every single day.

37
00:01:38,648 --> 00:01:40,774
So it spends a huge chunk of its life looking

38
00:01:40,774 --> 00:01:44,374
for things that move. And here's the thing about small prey.

39
00:01:44,374 --> 00:01:46,661
Sometimes a mouse plays dead.

40
00:01:46,661 --> 00:01:50,305
Sometimes a lizard freezes perfectly still against a rock.

41
00:01:50,305 --> 00:01:53,252
So a hunting cat picks up a very handy trick.

42
00:01:53,252 --> 00:01:54,212
When it finds something

43
00:01:54,212 --> 00:01:57,332
that might be alive it gives the thing a poke with its paw.

44
00:01:57,332 --> 00:01:59,558
If it runs then dinner is on.

45
00:01:59,558 --> 00:02:01,866
If it doesn't move then the cat pokes it again just

46
00:02:01,866 --> 00:02:04,002
to be sure. That little paw tap is

47
00:02:04,002 --> 00:02:06,400
one of the most basic moves a cat owns.

48
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:10,224
You can spot it in kittens long before they ever catch anything real.

49
00:02:10,224 --> 00:02:11,415
They bat at bits of string

50
00:02:11,415 --> 00:02:14,132
and dry leaves and their mother's twitching tail.

51
00:02:14,132 --> 00:02:17,831
They swat at their brothers and sisters until somebody squeaks.

52
00:02:17,831 --> 00:02:21,146
All of that is practice for the hunting they'll need to do later.

53
00:02:21,146 --> 00:02:24,812
The poke and the swat are wired deep into every single cat.

54
00:02:24,812 --> 00:02:27,638
Now picture your cat up on the kitchen counter.

55
00:02:27,638 --> 00:02:31,254
It comes across a small object it has never seen before.

56
00:02:31,254 --> 00:02:34,641
Maybe it's a bottle cap or a hair tie or your car keys.

57
00:02:34,641 --> 00:02:38,258
The hunter side of its brain asks one very simple question.

58
00:02:38,258 --> 00:02:40,741
Could this thing possibly be alive?

59
00:02:40,741 --> 00:02:43,743
And the quickest way to find out is to give it a tap.

60
00:02:43,743 --> 00:02:47,511
The object slides a little bit. Now that is pretty interesting.

61
00:02:47,511 --> 00:02:49,718
So the cat gives it another tap.

62
00:02:49,718 --> 00:02:52,763
It slides a bit further and then it drops off the edge.

63
00:02:52,763 --> 00:02:55,250
That's even more interesting to a cat.

64
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:57,871
Something moved and then it went somewhere new.

65
00:02:57,871 --> 00:03:02,019
For a hunter that's about as exciting as an ordinary afternoon gets.

66
00:03:02,019 --> 00:03:05,295
There's another reason cats lean so hard on their paws.

67
00:03:05,295 --> 00:03:08,046
Cats don't actually see very well up close.

68
00:03:08,046 --> 00:03:12,014
Their eyes are built for spotting movement across a room or a field.

69
00:03:12,014 --> 00:03:15,493
Things sitting right under their nose tend to look a bit blurry.

70
00:03:15,493 --> 00:03:19,296
So for close up work a cat leans on its other senses instead.

71
00:03:19,296 --> 00:03:22,035
Their paws are packed with sensitive nerves.

72
00:03:22,035 --> 00:03:25,625
Their whiskers can pick up tiny changes in the air around them.

73
00:03:25,625 --> 00:03:27,484
Cats even have a few stiff whiskers

74
00:03:27,484 --> 00:03:29,304
on the backs of their front legs.

75
00:03:29,304 --> 00:03:32,792
Those help them feel what's going on right under their own chin.

76
00:03:32,792 --> 00:03:34,308
So when your cat pats at something

77
00:03:34,308 --> 00:03:37,201
on the table it's partly just getting a better look.

78
00:03:37,201 --> 00:03:40,174
It's feeling the thing out to learn what it actually is.

79
00:03:40,174 --> 00:03:42,419
There's also some evidence that cats are trying

80
00:03:42,419 --> 00:03:44,528
to work out how the world behaves.

81
00:03:44,528 --> 00:03:48,606
In 2016 a team of researchers at Kyoto University

82
00:03:48,606 --> 00:03:50,940
in Japan ran a clever little test.

83
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:53,741
They shook a closed container in front of cats.

84
00:03:53,741 --> 00:03:57,363
Sometimes the container rattled like there was something inside.

85
00:03:57,363 --> 00:03:59,743
Sometimes it made no sound at all.

86
00:03:59,743 --> 00:04:01,600
Then they tipped it over.

87
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:05,504
Sometimes an object fell out and sometimes nothing came out at all.

88
00:04:05,504 --> 00:04:09,154
The cats stared longer whenever the result didn't match the sound.

89
00:04:09,154 --> 00:04:12,641
A rattling container that dropped nothing seemed to puzzle them.

90
00:04:12,641 --> 00:04:16,075
A silent one that suddenly dropped something puzzled them too.

91
00:04:16,075 --> 00:04:17,919
The researchers took this as a sign

92
00:04:17,919 --> 00:04:20,571
that cats understand some cause and effect.

93
00:04:20,571 --> 00:04:22,252
They seem to carry a rough idea

94
00:04:22,252 --> 00:04:25,840
that a rattle means something is inside. And they seem to expect

95
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:28,516
that things fall down when nothing holds them up.

96
00:04:28,516 --> 00:04:31,918
So maybe your cat is running little experiments of its own.

97
00:04:31,918 --> 00:04:34,234
What happens if I push this pen?

98
00:04:34,234 --> 00:04:37,613
The pen falls right off. What about this shiny spoon?

99
00:04:37,613 --> 00:04:40,207
It falls too and makes a lovely clatter.

100
00:04:40,207 --> 00:04:42,890
Does the remote control fall as well?

101
00:04:42,890 --> 00:04:45,450
It certainly does and it bounces too.

102
00:04:45,450 --> 00:04:47,872
Every test comes back with the same answer

103
00:04:47,872 --> 00:04:50,196
and somehow it never seems to get old.

104
00:04:50,196 --> 00:04:52,809
And honestly the noise might be half the fun.

105
00:04:52,809 --> 00:04:56,001
A pen hitting a wooden floor makes a sharp little click.

106
00:04:56,001 --> 00:04:58,434
A spoon makes a bright ringing clatter.

107
00:04:58,434 --> 00:05:01,222
A set of keys makes a great jangly crash.

108
00:05:01,222 --> 00:05:03,683
To an animal with ears as good as a cat's

109
00:05:03,683 --> 00:05:06,293
that's a whole lot of interesting information.

110
00:05:06,293 --> 00:05:08,343
It's a bit like a toddler dropping a spoon

111
00:05:08,343 --> 00:05:10,383
from a high chair over and over.

112
00:05:10,383 --> 00:05:12,725
The sound is part of the reward.

113
00:05:12,725 --> 00:05:16,132
But the hunting instinct doesn't explain the most famous part.

114
00:05:16,132 --> 00:05:19,364
It doesn't explain the stare. Why does the cat look straight at you

115
00:05:19,364 --> 00:05:21,640
while it nudges the mug toward the edge?

116
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:24,296
This is the part that's really all about you.

117
00:05:24,296 --> 00:05:27,232
Cats are very quick to learn what gets a reaction.

118
00:05:27,232 --> 00:05:28,973
Think about what happens the first time

119
00:05:28,973 --> 00:05:31,193
a cat knocks something off a shelf.

120
00:05:31,193 --> 00:05:33,248
You jump up off the sofa.

121
00:05:33,248 --> 00:05:35,760
You call its name in a loud voice.

122
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:38,032
You rush over and pick the thing up.

123
00:05:38,032 --> 00:05:40,609
Maybe you scoop up the cat while you're at it.

124
00:05:40,609 --> 00:05:44,348
For a few seconds you're giving it your complete and total attention.

125
00:05:44,348 --> 00:05:47,272
From the cat's side of things that's a great result.

126
00:05:47,272 --> 00:05:50,338
It was bored and now you're up and moving and talking.

127
00:05:50,338 --> 00:05:53,183
It doesn't really matter to the cat that you're annoyed.

128
00:05:53,183 --> 00:05:56,277
Any attention at all still counts as attention.

129
00:05:56,277 --> 00:05:57,985
So the cat files away a simple

130
00:05:57,985 --> 00:06:00,456
lesson about pushing things off the table.

131
00:06:00,456 --> 00:06:03,214
It makes the human come running every time.

132
00:06:03,214 --> 00:06:05,640
And the next time it wants you it knows exactly

133
00:06:05,640 --> 00:06:07,081
which button to press.

134
00:06:07,081 --> 00:06:10,698
That's why so many cats do this when they want something specific.

135
00:06:10,698 --> 00:06:13,198
It happens a lot around mealtimes.

136
00:06:13,198 --> 00:06:16,236
The cat strolls along the dresser in the early morning.

137
00:06:16,236 --> 00:06:18,542
It looks over at you lying in bed.

138
00:06:18,542 --> 00:06:21,321
Then it sends your phone sliding onto the floor.

139
00:06:21,321 --> 00:06:25,042
That's not some random accident. That's a cat that has worked out how

140
00:06:25,042 --> 00:06:27,379
to get you out of bed and into the kitchen.

141
00:06:27,379 --> 00:06:30,376
It also happens when you're busy with something else.

142
00:06:30,376 --> 00:06:31,862
Have you noticed that cats love

143
00:06:31,862 --> 00:06:33,878
to clear your desk while you're working?

144
00:06:33,878 --> 00:06:36,751
You're staring at a screen and you're ignoring them.

145
00:06:36,751 --> 00:06:40,296
So they stroll across the keyboard or push your pen off the edge.

146
00:06:40,296 --> 00:06:42,562
Suddenly you're looking at them again.

147
00:06:42,562 --> 00:06:45,671
As far as the cat is concerned that worked perfectly.

148
00:06:45,671 --> 00:06:48,876
A lot of experts also point to plain old boredom.

149
00:06:48,876 --> 00:06:52,358
An indoor cat has a hunter's body and a hunter's brain.

150
00:06:52,358 --> 00:06:55,596
But plenty of indoor cats have almost nothing to hunt.

151
00:06:55,596 --> 00:06:58,277
They sleep and eat and stare out of the window.

152
00:06:58,277 --> 00:07:00,110
Knocking things over is fun

153
00:07:00,110 --> 00:07:02,707
and it uses every one of those hunting moves.

154
00:07:02,707 --> 00:07:04,329
If a cat doesn't get enough play

155
00:07:04,329 --> 00:07:06,618
it will happily invent its own games.

156
00:07:06,618 --> 00:07:09,600
And your belongings make very convenient toys.

157
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:12,287
Young cats tend to do this more than old ones.

158
00:07:12,287 --> 00:07:16,372
A kitten or a young adult cat has a ridiculous amount of energy.

159
00:07:16,372 --> 00:07:20,498
A senior cat usually prefers a sunny windowsill and a long nap.

160
00:07:20,498 --> 00:07:22,630
So if your young cat is clearing every shelf

161
00:07:22,630 --> 00:07:25,400
in the house it might simply need more to do.

162
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:28,493
Sometimes the reason is even simpler than that.

163
00:07:28,493 --> 00:07:31,501
The cat wants to be exactly where the object is.

164
00:07:31,501 --> 00:07:35,303
Cats love lying on tables and shelves and desks. If your book is

165
00:07:35,303 --> 00:07:38,158
in the way the cat may just shove it aside to make room.

166
00:07:38,158 --> 00:07:41,236
In that case the falling part isn't the point at all.

167
00:07:41,236 --> 00:07:44,343
It's more like tidying up in a very cat sort of way.

168
00:07:44,343 --> 00:07:46,044
And there's a good reason your cat wants

169
00:07:46,044 --> 00:07:47,976
to be up there in the first place.

170
00:07:47,976 --> 00:07:49,994
Cats love high places.

171
00:07:49,994 --> 00:07:52,485
Out in the wild a small cat is a hunter

172
00:07:52,485 --> 00:07:54,717
but it's also somebody else's prey.

173
00:07:54,717 --> 00:07:58,204
Foxes and eagles and bigger cats would all happily eat it.

174
00:07:58,204 --> 00:08:00,558
So a high perch is a safe perch.

175
00:08:00,558 --> 00:08:02,762
From up there a cat can see everything coming

176
00:08:02,762 --> 00:08:05,660
and nothing can sneak up on it. Your kitchen counter

177
00:08:05,660 --> 00:08:08,914
and your bookshelf are the living room version of a tree branch.

178
00:08:08,914 --> 00:08:11,661
So the cat climbs up to its lookout spot.

179
00:08:11,661 --> 00:08:14,775
And anything sitting on the lookout spot is fair game.

180
00:08:14,775 --> 00:08:16,611
So how do you stop it?

181
00:08:16,611 --> 00:08:19,380
Or at least how do you make it happen a little less?

182
00:08:19,380 --> 00:08:22,320
The first rule is not to punish your cat for it.

183
00:08:22,320 --> 00:08:23,947
Yelling at a cat or spraying it

184
00:08:23,947 --> 00:08:27,436
with water doesn't teach it that knocking things over is wrong.

185
00:08:27,436 --> 00:08:30,272
It just teaches the cat that you can be scary.

186
00:08:30,272 --> 00:08:32,997
And in some cases the yelling was the reward the cat was

187
00:08:32,997 --> 00:08:37,034
after anyway. The easiest fix is also the most boring one.

188
00:08:37,034 --> 00:08:39,090
Put the precious stuff away.

189
00:08:39,090 --> 00:08:42,654
Keep glasses and mugs well back from the edge of the table.

190
00:08:42,654 --> 00:08:46,360
Tuck anything breakable inside a cabinet. If there's nothing fragile

191
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:49,848
to knock over then a knocked over object is no big deal.

192
00:08:49,848 --> 00:08:51,748
Some people use a little museum putty

193
00:08:51,748 --> 00:08:54,400
to stick ornaments down so they can't budge.

194
00:08:54,400 --> 00:08:57,444
The second fix is to give your cat a better hunt.

195
00:08:57,444 --> 00:08:59,476
Play with it every day using a wand toy

196
00:08:59,476 --> 00:09:02,101
with feathers or a toy mouse on a string.

197
00:09:02,101 --> 00:09:04,320
Let it stalk and chase and pounce.

198
00:09:04,320 --> 00:09:05,852
Let it catch the toy at the end

199
00:09:05,852 --> 00:09:07,844
so it feels like a real win.

200
00:09:07,844 --> 00:09:11,292
Plenty of cat experts suggest playing right before a meal.

201
00:09:11,292 --> 00:09:12,908
That way the cat gets to hunt

202
00:09:12,908 --> 00:09:15,474
and then eat just like a wild cat would.

203
00:09:15,474 --> 00:09:18,226
A cat that has had a good hunt is usually much happier

204
00:09:18,226 --> 00:09:19,882
to curl up and snooze.

205
00:09:19,882 --> 00:09:22,367
Puzzle feeders help a lot as well.

206
00:09:22,367 --> 00:09:25,511
These are toys that hold dry food or a few treats.

207
00:09:25,511 --> 00:09:27,024
The cat has to bat and roll

208
00:09:27,024 --> 00:09:29,067
and paw at them to get the food out.

209
00:09:29,067 --> 00:09:32,506
That turns all that swatting energy into something useful.

210
00:09:32,506 --> 00:09:36,788
Some cats will happily spend ages chasing a treat ball across the floor.

211
00:09:36,788 --> 00:09:38,402
You can also give your cat a shelf

212
00:09:38,402 --> 00:09:41,255
with its very own things on it. A few safe toys

213
00:09:41,255 --> 00:09:44,130
on a low shelf can become its private knocking spot.

214
00:09:44,130 --> 00:09:47,383
If something falls off there then it really doesn't matter.

215
00:09:47,383 --> 00:09:49,788
And when your cat does push something over try not

216
00:09:49,788 --> 00:09:51,156
to make a big scene.

217
00:09:51,156 --> 00:09:53,644
Stay calm and pick it up a bit later.

218
00:09:53,644 --> 00:09:56,336
If you rush over every single time the cat learns

219
00:09:56,336 --> 00:09:57,662
that the trick works.

220
00:09:57,662 --> 00:10:01,296
If nothing exciting happens the game slowly gets less fun.

221
00:10:01,296 --> 00:10:04,918
Then make sure your cat gets plenty of attention at other times too.

222
00:10:04,918 --> 00:10:06,655
Give it that attention when it's calm

223
00:10:06,655 --> 00:10:09,652
and gentle and not only when it's causing trouble.

224
00:10:09,652 --> 00:10:12,148
There's one more thing worth saying here.

225
00:10:12,148 --> 00:10:15,240
A cat that suddenly starts knocking things over far more

226
00:10:15,240 --> 00:10:17,930
than usual might be trying to tell you something.

227
00:10:17,930 --> 00:10:20,793
It could be bored after a change in your routine.

228
00:10:20,793 --> 00:10:23,589
It could be hungry because its food has changed.

229
00:10:23,589 --> 00:10:27,464
Older cats sometimes act differently when their eyesight starts to fade.

230
00:10:27,464 --> 00:10:28,636
If the change is big

231
00:10:28,636 --> 00:10:31,687
and sudden it does no harm to mention it to your vet.

232
00:10:31,687 --> 00:10:34,508
So why does your cat knock things off the table?

233
00:10:34,508 --> 00:10:35,815
Because it's a hunter at heart

234
00:10:35,815 --> 00:10:39,227
and a quick tap is how hunters check if something is alive.

235
00:10:39,227 --> 00:10:43,157
Because its eyes aren't great up close and its paws help it explore.

236
00:10:43,157 --> 00:10:47,274
Because it seems curious about how things fall and what noise they make.

237
00:10:47,274 --> 00:10:48,182
Because it has learned

238
00:10:48,182 --> 00:10:51,477
that a falling object makes you jump up and pay attention.

239
00:10:51,477 --> 00:10:53,602
And because sometimes it's just plain bored

240
00:10:53,602 --> 00:10:55,939
and your stuff makes a really fun toy.

241
00:10:55,939 --> 00:10:57,561
So the next time a paw slides

242
00:10:57,561 --> 00:11:00,459
your mug toward the edge take a breath before you shout.

243
00:11:00,459 --> 00:11:02,923
Look at the cat looking right back at you.

244
00:11:02,923 --> 00:11:05,899
It isn't being evil and it isn't out to get you.

245
00:11:05,899 --> 00:11:09,296
It's a tiny desert hunter stuck inside a living room.

246
00:11:09,296 --> 00:11:12,396
And right now it mostly just wants you to come and play.
